  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nThe [spec](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/semantics-other.html#selector-active) requires using this first relevant entry to determine if an element is being activated:\n\n  > [...] an HTML user agent must use the first relevant entry in the following list\n  >\n  > - **[...]**\n  > - **If the element is focusable**\n  >\n  > The element is being activated if it is in a formal activation state.\n\nIf I am not mistaken, `<div tabindex=\"1\">focusable</div>` cannot be in a formal activation state because it has no activation behavior. Therefore, the user agent should not check the remaining entry of the algorithm:\n\n  > - **If the element is being actively pointed at**\n  >\n  > The element is being activated.\n\nHowever, it matches `:active` in current version of Chrome stable and Firefox ESR, when it is actively pointed at.\n\n",
